1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:15,891
In June 1462, the army of the Ottoman sultan, Mehmed the Second, marched on Târgoviște, the capital of a small Christian principality called Wallachia, in what is now Romania.

2
00:00:16,991 --> 00:00:27,785
Outside the city, according to a Greek historian of the time, the sultan's soldiers came upon a field of stakes, where thousands of prisoners had been put to death.

3
00:00:28,885 --> 00:00:41,003
The prince responsible was Vlad the Third. In a letter that still survives, he counted twenty-three thousand, eight hundred and eighty-four people killed in a single winter campaign.

4
00:00:42,103 --> 00:00:54,753
Today the world knows him by another name: Dracula. But the real man was no vampire, and in Romania many still remember him as a hero. This is how both reputations were made.

5
00:00:56,953 --> 00:01:08,239
Vlad was born around 1431, probably in the Transylvanian town of Sighișoara, which was then part of the Kingdom of Hungary.

6
00:01:09,339 --> 00:01:21,157
His father, Vlad the Second, had been admitted to the Order of the Dragon, a brotherhood of Christian nobles sworn to fight the Ottoman Turks. He took the name Dracul, the Dragon.

7
00:01:22,257 --> 00:01:32,476
So his son became Dracula: son of the Dragon. In Romanian, the word drac can also mean devil, which only darkened the name over time.

8
00:01:33,576 --> 00:01:44,648
Wallachia lay between two great powers: Catholic Hungary to the north, and the expanding Ottoman Empire to the south. Its princes survived by changing sides.

9
00:01:46,848 --> 00:01:59,776
In the early 1440s, to guarantee his own loyalty, Vlad's father sent two of his sons to the Ottoman sultan as hostages: Vlad and his younger brother, Radu.

10
00:02:00,876 --> 00:02:10,028
They spent several years at the Ottoman court and were educated there. Radu grew close to the Ottomans. Vlad, by most accounts, never did.

11
00:02:11,128 --> 00:02:27,768
In 1447, Hungarian forces under John Hunyadi, the regent of Hungary, invaded Wallachia. Vlad's father was killed, and his elder brother, Mircea, was murdered by the boyars, Wallachia's powerful nobles.

12
00:02:28,868 --> 00:02:38,383
In 1448, still a young man, Vlad seized the throne with Ottoman help. He lost it again within about two months.

13
00:02:40,583 --> 00:02:52,231
After years in exile, he took power again in 1456, this time with Hungarian backing. This, his main reign, lasted six years.

14
00:02:53,331 --> 00:03:02,611
His best-known portrait, a copy made long after his death, shows a stern man with long dark hair, a heavy moustache and a jewelled cap.

15
00:03:03,711 --> 00:03:15,721
He set out to break the power of the boyars, the noble families who had made and unmade princes. Many were executed, and their lands and offices went to men loyal to him.

16
00:03:16,821 --> 00:03:27,701
A later story says he seized nobles at an Easter feast and forced them to rebuild his mountain fortress at Poenari. Historians treat it with caution.

17
00:03:29,901 --> 00:03:39,352
His preferred punishment was death on a sharpened stake. It later gave him the name Romanians still use for him: Țepeș, the Impaler.

18
00:03:40,452 --> 00:03:46,468
He used it on thieves, rivals and prisoners of war. The point was terror, and it worked.

19
00:03:47,568 --> 00:03:59,984
He also raided the Saxon towns of Transylvania, German-speaking trading cities such as Brașov and Sibiu, which had sheltered his rivals. Townspeople and villagers were killed.

20
00:04:01,084 --> 00:04:15,548
Survivors carried their stories west. German pamphlets about him, first written in the 1460s and later printed in many editions, became some of the earliest bestsellers of the printing age.

21
00:04:16,648 --> 00:04:30,771
Historians read these pamphlets carefully. They were written by his enemies, often to sell copies, and some details are clearly exaggerated. But Ottoman, Greek and Russian writers describe the same cruelty.

22
00:04:31,871 --> 00:04:44,393
Romanian folk memory tells a different story: of a stern judge who stamped out theft so completely that a golden cup could be left at a public fountain, and no one dared to take it.

23
00:04:46,593 --> 00:04:58,732
The great power to the south was ruled by Mehmed the Second, the sultan who had conquered Constantinople in 1453. Vlad stopped paying him tribute.

24
00:04:59,832 --> 00:05:14,723
In the winter of 1461, Vlad crossed the Danube into Ottoman territory and destroyed towns and forts along the river. This was the campaign he described in his letter to the king of Hungary.

25
00:05:15,823 --> 00:05:29,497
In the summer of 1462, Mehmed invaded Wallachia with a far larger army. Vlad could not face him in open battle, so he burned the crops, poisoned wells and fell back.

26
00:05:30,597 --> 00:05:38,981
In June, he led a night raid into the Ottoman camp, hoping to kill the sultan himself. It caused chaos, but it failed.

27
00:05:40,081 --> 00:05:55,292
Then the sultan reached Târgoviște and the field of stakes. The Greek historian Laonikos Chalkokondyles put the number of dead there at about twenty thousand. Even if that is exaggerated, the sight shook the invaders.

28
00:05:57,492 --> 00:06:08,393
But Mehmed did not need to win a battle. He placed Vlad's brother Radu, who had stayed loyal to the Ottomans, on the Wallachian throne, and many nobles went over to him.

29
00:06:09,493 --> 00:06:20,373
Vlad fled to Hungary to seek help from its young king, Matthias Corvinus. Instead, Matthias had him arrested, and kept him a prisoner for about twelve years.

30
00:06:21,473 --> 00:06:33,100
Released at last, he married a cousin of King Matthias, and in 1476 he fought his way back onto the throne of Wallachia, for the third time.

31
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:44,653
Within weeks, around the turn of the year, he was killed in battle against the Ottomans and their Wallachian allies. Accounts of exactly how he died differ.

32
00:06:45,753 --> 00:06:58,169
According to several accounts, his head was sent to the sultan in Constantinople. Where his body lies is unknown. The monastery at Snagov, long named as his grave, is disputed.

33
00:07:00,369 --> 00:07:07,239
Four centuries later, an Irish theatre manager named Bram Stoker was working on a vampire novel.

34
00:07:08,339 --> 00:07:21,864
In 1890, in a library in the English seaside town of Whitby, he read an old book about Wallachia, which claimed that Dracula meant devil. He took the name for his villain.

35
00:07:22,964 --> 00:07:35,700
Dracula was published in 1897. Scholars who have studied Stoker's working notes find that he knew very little about the real Vlad beyond his name.

36
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:47,701
Count Dracula is a fictional Transylvanian nobleman. Vlad was a Wallachian prince, and nothing in the historical record connects him to vampires.

37
00:07:48,801 --> 00:07:59,361
Even so, tourists now visit Bran Castle in Transylvania as Dracula's castle, though there is little evidence that Vlad ever spent time there.

38
00:08:01,561 --> 00:08:12,548
In Romania, the picture is different. Many remember him as a defender of his country against the Ottomans, and a ruler who brought harsh order to a lawless age.

39
00:08:13,648 --> 00:08:23,547
Communist Romania celebrated him as a national hero, and statues and busts of him still stand in towns associated with his life.

40
00:08:24,647 --> 00:08:36,103
Some modern writers estimate that he killed tens of thousands of people. Those figures rest largely on hostile sources, but even the numbers in his own letter are grim.

41
00:08:37,203 --> 00:08:47,016
There are really two Draculas. One is a fictional count who never lived. The other was a real prince, brutal even by the standards of a brutal age.

42
00:08:48,116 --> 00:08:58,612
The vampire is the one people remember. But the real man needed no legend to be frightening. His enemies, and his own letters, recorded that well enough.
